

[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mPath: architectural.[0m A notifications system is a new subsystem — new data, new delivery path, new interfaces — so I'll
  do the full process: questions, approaches, a sectioned design, and a written spec before any code.

  Before questions, one thing I have to flag, because it changes what we're actually designing.

  [1mThis repo has no app yet.[0m The entire contents:

  [90m<[34mh1[90m>[39mTasks[90m</[34mh1[90m>[39m
  [90m<[34mmain[90m></[34mmain[90m>[39m

  One static HTML file, one commit ("initial: empty tasks page"). There is no task model, no storage, no users, no
  server, no concept of "change." A notification system is downstream of all four — "notify users when tasks they care
  about change" presupposes that tasks exist, that users exist and are distinguishable, that "care about" is a recorded
  relationship, and that changes are observable events somewhere. None of those exist here.

  So there are really two possibilities, and they lead to very different work:

  - [1mThis is a stub of a real app that lives elsewhere.[0m Then the design is genuinely about notifications, and I need to
    see that codebase — its task model and where mutations happen determine nearly every decision below.
  - [1mThis repo is the app, and it's a greenfield start.[0m Then "notifications" is not the first subsystem to build. It's
    the fourth. We'd need to decompose: tasks + storage, then identity, then a change/event representation, then
    notifications on top. Each of those gets its own spec → plan → implementation cycle, and I'd brainstorm the first
    one with you now — not notifications.

  I don't want to spend clarifying questions refining delivery channels and digest windows for a system whose
  foundations may not be decided yet.

